f="https://doi.org/10.1109%2FMAP.2010.5723273" class="external text" id="mwCB8">10.1109/MAP.2010.5723273</a>. <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/ISSN_(identifier)" title="ISSN (identifier)" class="mw-redirect" id="mwCCA">ISSN</a><span typeof="mw:Entity" id="mwCCE"> </span><a rel="mw:ExtLink nofollow" href="https://search.worldcat.org/issn/1045-9243" class="external text" id="mwCCI">1045-9243</a>.</cite><span title="ctx_ver=Z39.88-2004&amp;rft_val_fmt=info%3Aofi%2Ffmt%3Akev%3Amtx%3Ajournal&amp;rft.genre=article&amp;rft.jtitle=IEEE+Antennas+and+Propagation+Magazine&amp;rft.atitle=%22c%22+Utrum+est+ut+Celeritas+an+Constantia%3F+%28Does+%22c%22+Stand+for+Speed+or+Constancy%3F%29&amp;rft.volume=52&amp;rft.issue=6&amp;rft.pages=207-219&amp;rft.date=2010-12&amp;rft.issn=1045-9243&amp;rft_id=info%3Adoi%2F10.1109%2FMAP.2010.5723273&amp;rft_id=info%3Abibcode%2F2010IAPM...52..207P&amp;rft.aulast=Pelosi&amp;rft.aufirst=G.&amp;rft.au=Selleri%2C+S.&amp;rfr_id=info%3Asid%2Fen.wikipedia.org%3ASpeed+of+light" class="Z3988" about="#mwt99" id="mwCCM"></span></span></li>
<li about="#cite_note-Yc-15" id="cite_note-Yc-15" data-mw-footnote-number="13"><span class="mw-cite-backlink" id="mwCCQ"><a href="#cite_ref-Yc_15-0" rel="mw:referencedBy" id="mwCCU"><span class="mw-linkback-text" id="mwCCY">↑</span></a></span> <span id="mw-reference-text-cite_note-Yc-15" class="mw-reference-text reference-text">
<link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1333433106" about="#mwt102" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles mw:Transclusion" id="mwCCc" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Module:Citation/CS1/styles.css"},"body":{"extsrc":""},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"Cite web\n ","href":"./Template:Cite_web"},"params":{"last":{"wt":"Gibbs"},"first":{"wt":"P."},"year":{"wt":"2004"},"orig-date":{"wt":"1997"},"title":{"wt":"Why is &apos;&apos;c&apos;&apos; the symbol for the speed of light?"},"url":{"wt":"http://math.ucr.edu/home/baez/physics/Relativity/SpeedOfLight/c.html"},"work":{"wt":"Usenet Physics FAQ"},"publisher":{"wt":"[[University of California, Riverside]]"},"access-date":{"wt":"16 November 2009"},"archive-url":{"wt":"https://web.archive.org/web/20100325220247/http://math.ucr.edu/home/baez/physics/Relativity/SpeedOfLight/c.html"},"archive-date":{"wt":"25 March 2010"}},"i":0}}]}'/><cite id="CITEREFGibbs2004" class="citation web cs1" about="#mwt102">Gibbs, P. (2004) [1997]. <a rel="mw:ExtLink nofollow" href="https://web.archive.org/web/20100325220247/http://math.ucr.edu/home/baez/physics/Relativity/SpeedOfLight/c.html" class="external text" id="mwCCg">"Why is <i id="mwCCk">c</i> the symbol for the speed of light?"</a>. <i id="mwCCo">Usenet Physics FAQ</i>. <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/University_of_California,_Riverside" title="University of California, Riverside" id="mwCCs">University of California, Riverside</a>. Archived from <a rel="mw:ExtLink nofollow" href="http://math.ucr.edu/home/baez/physics/Relativity/SpeedOfLight/c.html" class="external text" id="mwCCw">the original</a> on 25 March 2010<span class="reference-accessdate" id="mwCC0">. Retrieved <span class="nowrap" id="mwCC4">16 November</span> 2009</span>.</cite><span title="ctx_ver=Z39.88-2004&amp;rft_val_fmt=info%3Aofi%2Ffmt%3Akev%3Amtx%3Ajournal&amp;rft.genre=unknown&amp;rft.jtitle=Usenet+Physics+FAQ&amp;rft.atitle=Why+is+c+the+symbol+for+the+speed+of+light%3F&amp;rft.date=2004&amp;rft.aulast=Gibbs&amp;rft.aufirst=P.&amp;rft_id=http%3A%2F%2Fmath.ucr.edu%2Fhome%2Fbaez%2Fphysics%2FRelativity%2FSpeedOfLight%2Fc.html&amp;rfr_id=info%3Asid%2Fen.wikipedia.org%3ASpeed+of+light" class="Z3988" about="#mwt102" id="mwCC8"></span>
"The origins of the letter c being used for the speed of light can be traced back to a paper of 1856 by Weber and Kohlrausch [...] Weber apparently meant c to stand for 'constant' in his force law, but there is evidence that physicists such as Lorentz and Einstein were accustomed to a common convention that c could be used as a variable for velocity. This usage can be traced back to the classic Latin texts in which c stood for 'celeritas', meaning 'speed'."
</span></li>
