<li about="#cite_note-nist-definitions-17" id="cite_note-nist-definitions-17" data-mw-footnote-number="15"><span rel="mw:referencedBy" class="mw-cite-backlink" id="mwCGI"><a href="#cite_ref-nist-definitions_17-0" id="mwCGM"><span class="mw-linkback-text" id="mwCGQ">1</span></a> <a href="#cite_ref-nist-definitions_17-1" id="mwCGU"><span class="mw-linkback-text" id="mwCGY">2</span></a></span> <span id="mw-reference-text-cite_note-nist-definitions-17" class="mw-reference-text reference-text"><link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1333433106" about="#mwt128" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles mw:Transclusion" id="mwCGc" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Module:Citation/CS1/styles.css"},"body":{"extsrc":""},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"Cite web ","href":"./Template:Cite_web"},"params":{"url":{"wt":"https://physics.nist.gov/cuu/Units/current.html"},"title":{"wt":"Definitions of the SI base units"},"website":{"wt":"physics.nist.gov"},"date":{"wt":"29 May 2019"},"access-date":{"wt":"8 February 2022"}},"i":0}}]}'/><cite class="citation web cs1" about="#mwt128" id="mwCGg"><a rel="mw:ExtLink nofollow" href="https://physics.nist.gov/cuu/Units/current.html" class="external text" id="mwCGk">"Definitions of the SI base units"</a>. <i id="mwCGo">physics.nist.gov</i>. 29 May 2019<span class="reference-accessdate" id="mwCGs">. Retrieved <span class="nowrap" id="mwCGw">8 February</span> 2022</span>.</cite><span title="ctx_ver=Z39.88-2004&amp;rft_val_fmt=info%3Aofi%2Ffmt%3Akev%3Amtx%3Ajournal&amp;rft.genre=unknown&amp;rft.jtitle=physics.nist.gov&amp;rft.atitle=Definitions+of+the+SI+base+units&amp;rft.date=2019-05-29&amp;rft_id=https%3A%2F%2Fphysics.nist.gov%2Fcuu%2FUnits%2Fcurrent.html&amp;rfr_id=info%3Asid%2Fen.wikipedia.org%3ASpeed+of+light" class="Z3988" about="#mwt128" id="mwCG0"></span></span></li>
<li about="#cite_note-fixes-18" id="cite_note-fixes-18" data-mw-footnote-number="16"><span rel="mw:referencedBy" class="mw-cite-backlink" id="mwCG4"><a href="#cite_ref-fixes_18-0" id="mwCG8"><span class="mw-linkback-text" id="mwCHA">1</span></a> <a href="#cite_ref-fixes_18-1" id="mwCHE"><span class="mw-linkback-text" id="mwCHI">2</span></a></span> <span id="mw-reference-text-cite_note-fixes-18" class="mw-reference-text reference-text">See, for example:
<ul id="mwCHM"><li id="mwCHQ"><link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1333433106" about="#mwt131" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles mw:Transclusion" id="mwCHU" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Module:Citation/CS1/styles.css"},"body":{"extsrc":""},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"Cite book\n ","href":"./Template:Cite_book"},"params":{"last":{"wt":"Sydenham"},"first":{"wt":"P. H."},"year":{"wt":"2003"},"chapter":{"wt":"Measurement of length"},"chapter-url":{"wt":"https://books.google.com/books?id=sarHIbCVOUAC&amp;pg=PA56"},"editor":{"wt":"Boyes, W"},"title":{"wt":"Instrumentation Reference Book"},"edition":{"wt":"3"},"page":{"wt":"56"},"publisher":{"wt":"[[Butterworth–Heinemann]]"},"isbn":{"wt":"978-0-7506-7123-1"},"quote":{"wt":"...{{nbsp}}if the speed of light is defined as a fixed number then, in principle, the time standard will serve as the length standard{{nbsp}}..."}},"i":0}}]}'/><cite id="CITEREFSydenham2003" class="citation book cs1" about="#mwt131">Sydenham, P. H. (2003). <a rel="mw:ExtLink nofollow" href="https://books.google.com/books?id=sarHIbCVOUAC&amp;pg=PA56" class="external text" id="mwCHY">"Measurement of length"</a>. In Boyes, W (ed.). <i id="mwCHc">Instrumentation Reference Book</i> (3<span typeof="mw:Entity" id="mwCHg"> </span>ed.). <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Butterworth–Heinemann" title="Butterworth–Heinemann" class="mw-redirect" id="mwCHk">Butterworth–Heinemann</a>. p.<span typeof="mw:Entity" id="mwCHo"> </span>56. <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/ISBN_(identifier)" title="ISBN (identifier)" class="mw-redirect" id="mwCHs">ISBN</a><span typeof="mw:Entity" id="mwCHw"> </span><a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Special:BookSources/978-0-7506-7123-1" title="Special:BookSources/978-0-7506-7123-1" id="mwCH0"><bdi id="mwCH4">978-0-7506-7123-1</bdi></a>. <q id="mwCH8">...<span class="nowrap" id="mwCIA"><span typeof="mw:Entity" id="mwCIE"> </span></span>if the speed of light is defined as a fixed number then, in principle, the time standard will serve as the length standard<span class="nowrap" id="mwCII"><span typeof="mw:Entity" id="mwCIM"> </span></span>...</q></cite><span title="ctx_ver=Z39.88-2004&amp;rft_val_fmt=info%3Aofi%2Ffmt%3Akev%3Amtx%3Abook&amp;rft.genre=bookitem&amp;rft.atitle=Measurement+of+length&amp;rft.btitle=Instrumentation+Reference+Book&amp;rft.pages=56&amp;rft.edition=3&amp;rft.pub=Butterworth%E2%80%93Heinemann&amp;rft.date=2003&amp;rft.isbn=978-0-7506-7123-1&amp;rft.aulast=Sydenham&amp;rft.aufirst=P.+H.&amp;rft_id=https%3A%2F%2Fbooks.google.com%2Fbooks%3Fid%3DsarHIbCVOUAC%26pg%3DPA56&amp;rfr_id=info%3Asid%2Fen.wikipedia.org%3ASpeed+of+light" class="Z3988" about="#mwt131" id="mwCIQ"></span></li>
<li id="mwCIU"><link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1333433106" about="#mwt133" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles mw:Transclusion" id="mwCIY" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Module:Citation/CS1/styles.css"},"body":{"extsrc":""},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"Cite web\n ","href":"./Template:Cite_web"},"params":{"title":{"wt":"CODATA value: Speed of Light in Vacuum"},"url":{"wt":"http://physics.nist.gov/cgi-bin/cuu/Value?c"},"work":{"wt":"The NIST reference on Constants, Units, and Uncertainty"},"publisher":{"wt":"[[National Institute of Standards and Technology|NIST]]"},"access-date":{"wt":"21 August 2009"}},"i":0}}]}'/><cite class="citation web cs1" about="#mwt133" id="mwCIc"><a rel="mw:ExtLink nofollow" href="http://physics.nist.gov/cgi-bin/cuu/Value?c" class="external text" id="mwCIg">"CODATA value: Speed of Light in Vacuum"</a>. <i id="mwCIk">The NIST reference on Constants, Units, and Uncertainty</i>. <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/National_Institute_of_Standards_and_Technology" title="National Institute of Standards and Technology" id="mwCIo">NIST</a><span class="reference-accessdate" id="mwCIs">. Retrieved <span class="nowrap" id="mwCIw">21 August</span> 2009</span>.</cite><span title="ctx_ver=Z39.88-2004&amp;rft_val_fmt=info%3Aofi%2Ffmt%3Akev%3Amtx%3Ajournal&amp;rft.genre=unknown&amp;rft.jtitle=The+NIST+reference+on+Constants%2C+Units%2C+and+Uncertainty&amp;rft.atitle=CODATA+value%3A+Speed+of+Light+in+Vacuum&amp;rft_id=http%3A%2F%2Fphysics.nist.gov%2Fcgi-bin%2Fcuu%2FValue%3Fc&amp;rfr_id=info%3Asid%2Fen.wikipedia.org%3ASpeed+of+light" class="Z3988" about="#mwt133" id="mwCI0"></span></li>
<li id="mwCI4"><link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1333433106" about="#mwt135" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles mw:Transclusion" id="mwCI8" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Module:Citation/CS1/styles.css"},"body":{"extsrc":""},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"Cite book\n ","href":"./Template:Cite_book"},"params":{"last1":{"wt":"Jespersen"},"first1":{"wt":"J."},"last2":{"wt":"Fitz-Randolph"},"first2":{"wt":"J."},"last3":{"wt":"Robb"},"first3":{"wt":"J."},"year":{"wt":"1999"},"title":{"wt":"From Sundials to Atomic Clocks: Understanding Time and Frequency"},"url":{"wt":"https://books.google.com/books?id=Z7chuo4ebUAC&amp;pg=PA280"},"page":{"wt":"280"},"edition":{"wt":"Reprint of National Bureau of Standards 1977, 2nd"},"publisher":{"wt":"[[Courier Dover]]"},"isbn":{"wt":"978-0-486-40913-9"}},"i":0}}]}'/><cite id="CITEREFJespersenFitz-RandolphRobb1999" class="citation book cs1" about="#mwt135">Jespersen, J.; Fitz-Randolph, J.; Robb, J. (1999). <a rel="mw:ExtLink nofollow" href="https://books.google.com/books?id=Z7chuo4ebUAC&amp;pg=PA280" class="external text" id="mwCJA"><i id="mwCJE">From Sundials to Atomic Clocks: Understanding Time and Frequency</i></a> (Reprint of National Bureau of Standards 1977, 2nd<span typeof="mw:Entity" id="mwCJI"> </span>ed.). <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Courier_Dover" title="Courier Dover" class="mw-redirect" id="mwCJM">Courier Dover</a>. p.<span typeof="mw:Entity" id="mwCJQ"> </span>280. <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/ISBN_(identifier)" title="ISBN (identifier)" class="mw-redirect" id="mwCJU">ISBN</a><span typeof="mw:Entity" id="mwCJY"> </span><a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Special:BookSources/978-0-486-40913-9" title="Special:BookSources/978-0-486-40913-9" id="mwCJc"><bdi id="mwCJg">978-0-486-40913-9</bdi></a>.</cite><span title="ctx_ver=Z39.88-2004&amp;rft_val_fmt=info%3Aofi%2Ffmt%3Akev%3Amtx%3Abook&amp;rft.genre=book&amp;rft.btitle=From+Sundials+to+Atomic+Clocks%3A+Understanding+Time+and+Frequency&amp;rft.pages=280&amp;rft.edition=Reprint+of+National+Bureau+of+Standards+1977%2C+2nd&amp;rft.pub=Courier+Dover&amp;rft.date=1999&amp;rft.isbn=978-0-486-40913-9&amp;rft.aulast=Jespersen&amp;rft.aufirst=J.&amp;rft.au=Fitz-Randolph%2C+J.&amp;rft.au=Robb%2C+J.&amp;rft_id=https%3A%2F%2Fbooks.google.com%2Fbooks%3Fid%3DZ7chuo4ebUAC%26pg%3DPA280&amp;rfr_id=info%3Asid%2Fen.wikipedia.org%3ASpeed+of+light" class="Z3988" about="#mwt135" id="mwCJk"></span></li></ul></span></li>
<li about="#cite_note-penrose-19" id="cite_note-penrose-19" data-mw-footnote-number="17"><span class="mw-cite-backlink" id="mwCJo"><a href="#cite_ref-penrose_19-0" rel="mw:referencedBy" id="mwCJs"><span class="mw-linkback-text" id="mwCJw">↑</span></a></span> <span id="mw-reference-text-cite_note-penrose-19" class="mw-reference-text reference-text"><link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1333433106" about="#mwt139" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles mw:Transclusion" id="mwCJ0" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Module:Citation/CS1/styles.css"},"body":{"extsrc":""},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"Cite book ","href":"./Template:Cite_book"},"params":{"last":{"wt":"Penrose"},"first":{"wt":"R"},"author-link":{"wt":"Roger Penrose"},"year":{"wt":"2004"},"title":{"wt":"The Road to Reality: A Complete Guide to the Laws of the Universe"},"pages":{"wt":"[https://archive.org/details/roadtoreality00penr_319/page/n438 410]–411"},"publisher":{"wt":"Vintage Books"},"isbn":{"wt":"978-0-679-77631-4"},"quote":{"wt":"...{{nbsp}}the most accurate standard for the metre is conveniently &apos;&apos;defined&apos;&apos; so that there are exactly {{val|299792458}} of them to the distance travelled by light in a standard second, giving a value for the metre that very accurately matches the now inadequately precise [[History of the metre#International prototype metre|standard metre rule]] in Paris."},"title-link":{"wt":"The Road to Reality: A Complete Guide to the Laws of the Universe"}},"i":0}}]}'/><cite id="CITEREFPenrose2004" class="citation book cs1" about="#mwt139"><a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Roger_Penrose" title="Roger Penrose" id="mwCJ4">Penrose, R</a> (2004). <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Road_to_Reality:_A_Complete_Guide_to_the_Laws_of_the_Universe" title="The Road to Reality: A Complete Guide to the Laws of the Universe" class="mw-redirect" id="mwCJ8"><i id="mwCKA">The Road to Reality: A Complete Guide to the Laws of the Universe</i></a>. Vintage Books. pp.<span typeof="mw:Entity" id="mwCKE"> </span><a rel="mw:ExtLink nofollow" href="https://archive.org/details/roadtoreality00penr_319/page/n438" class="external text" id="mwCKI">410</a>–411. <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/ISBN_(identifier)" title="ISBN (identifier)" class="mw-redirect" id="mwCKM">ISBN</a><span typeof="mw:Entity" id="mwCKQ"> </span><a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Special:BookSources/978-0-679-77631-4" title="Special:BookSources/978-0-679-77631-4" id="mwCKU"><bdi id="mwCKY">978-0-679-77631-4</bdi></a>. <q id="mwCKc">...<span class="nowrap" id="mwCKg"><span typeof="mw:Entity" id="mwCKk"> </span></span>the most accurate standard for the metre is conveniently <i id="mwCKo">defined</i> so that there are exactly <span class="nowrap" id="mwCKs"><span data-sort-value="7008299792458000000♠" id="mwCKw"></span>299<span style="margin-left:.25em;" id="mwCK0">792</span><span style="margin-left:.25em;" id="mwCK4">458</span></span> of them to the distance travelled by light in a standard second, giving a value for the metre that very accurately matches the now inadequately precise <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/History_of_the_metre#International_prototype_metre" title="History of the metre" id="mwCK8">standard metre rule</a> in Paris.</q></cite><span title="ctx_ver=Z39.88-2004&amp;rft_val_fmt=info%3Aofi%2Ffmt%3Akev%3Amtx%3Abook&amp;rft.genre=book&amp;rft.btitle=The+Road+to+Reality%3A+A+Complete+Guide+to+the+Laws+of+the+Universe&amp;rft.pages=410-411&amp;rft.pub=Vintage+Books&amp;rft.date=2004&amp;rft.isbn=978-0-679-77631-4&amp;rft.aulast=Penrose&amp;rft.aufirst=R&amp;rfr_id=info%3Asid%2Fen.wikipedia.org%3ASpeed+of+light" class="Z3988" about="#mwt139" id="mwCLA"></span></span></li>
<li about="#cite_note-20" id="cite_note-20" data-mw-footnote-number="18"><span class="mw-cite-backlink" id="mwCLE"><a href="#cite_ref-20" rel="mw:referencedBy" id="mwCLI"><span class="mw-linkback-text" id="mwCLM">↑</span></a></span> <span id="mw-reference-text-cite_note-20" class="mw-reference-text reference-text"><link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1333433106" about="#mwt144" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles mw:Transclusion" id="mwCLQ" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Module:Citation/CS1/styles.css"},"body":{"extsrc":""},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"Cite book","href":"./Template:Cite_book"},"params":{"last":{"wt":"Mermin"},"first":{"wt":"N. David"},"title":{"wt":"It&apos;s About Time: Understanding Einstein&apos;s Relativity"},"date":{"wt":"2005"},"publisher":{"wt":"Princeton University Press"},"isbn":{"wt":"0-691-12201-6"},"location":{"wt":"Princeton"},"oclc":{"wt":"57283944"},"author-link":{"wt":"N. David Mermin"},"page":{"wt":"21"}},"i":0}}]}'/><cite id="CITEREFMermin2005" class="citation book cs1" about="#mwt144"><a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/N._David_Mermin" title="N. David Mermin" id="mwCLU">Mermin, N. David</a> (2005). <i id="mwCLY">It's About Time: Understanding Einstein's Relativity</i>. Princeton: Princeton University Press. p.<span typeof="mw:Entity" id="mwCLc"> </span>21. <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/ISBN_(identifier)" title="ISBN (identifier)" class="mw-redirect" id="mwCLg">ISBN</a><span typeof="mw:Entity" id="mwCLk"> </span><a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Special:BookSources/0-691-12201-6" title="Special:BookSources/0-691-12201-6" id="mwCLo"><bdi id="mwCLs">0-691-12201-6</bdi></a>. <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/OCLC_(identifier)" title="OCLC (identifier)" class="mw-redirect" id="mwCLw">OCLC</a><span typeof="mw:Entity" id="mwCL0"> </span><a rel="mw:ExtLink nofollow" href="https://search.worldcat.org/oclc/57283944" class="external text" id="mwCL4">57283944</a>.</cite><span title="ctx_ver=Z39.88-2004&amp;rft_val_fmt=info%3Aofi%2Ffmt%3Akev%3Amtx%3Abook&amp;rft.genre=book&amp;rft.btitle=It%27s+About+Time%3A+Understanding+Einstein%27s+Relativity&amp;rft.place=Princeton&amp;rft.pages=21&amp;rft.pub=Princeton+University+Press&amp;rft.date=2005&amp;rft_id=info%3Aoclcnum%2F57283944&amp;rft.isbn=0-691-12201-6&amp;rft.aulast=Mermin&amp;rft.aufirst=N.+David&amp;rfr_id=info%3Asid%2Fen.wikipedia.org%3ASpeed+of+light" class="Z3988" about="#mwt144" id="mwCL8"></span></span></li>
<li about="#cite_note-21" id="cite_note-21" data-mw-footnote-number="19"><span class="mw-cite-backlink" id="mwCMA"><a href="#cite_ref-21" rel="mw:referencedBy" id="mwCME"><span class="mw-linkback-text" id="mwCMI">↑</span></a></span> <span id="mw-reference-text-cite_note-21" class="mw-reference-text reference-text"><link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1333433106" about="#mwt147" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles mw:Transclusion" id="mwCMM" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Module:Citation/CS1/styles.css"},"body":{"extsrc":""},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"Cite web","href":"./Template:Cite_web"},"params":{"url":{"wt":"https://americanhistory.si.edu/collections/search/object/nmah_692464"},"title":{"wt":"Nanoseconds Associated with Grace Hopper"},"website":{"wt":"[[National Museum of American History]]"},"quote":{"wt":"[[Grace Hopper|Grace Murray Hopper]] (1906–1992), a mathematician who became a naval officer and computer scientist during World War II, started distributing these wire \"nanoseconds\" in the late 1960s in order to demonstrate how designing smaller components would produce faster computers."},"access-date":{"wt":"1 March 2022"}},"i":0}}]}'/><cite class="citation web cs1" about="#mwt147" id="mwCMQ"><a rel="mw:ExtLink nofollow" href="https://americanhistory.si.edu/collections/search/object/nmah_692464" class="external text" id="mwCMU">"Nanoseconds Associated with Grace Hopper"</a>. <i id="mwCMY"><a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/National_Museum_of_American_History" title="National Museum of American History" id="mwCMc">National Museum of American History</a></i><span class="reference-accessdate" id="mwCMg">. Retrieved <span class="nowrap" id="mwCMk">1 March</span> 2022</span>. <q id="mwCMo"><a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Grace_Hopper" title="Grace Hopper" id="mwCMs">Grace Murray Hopper</a> (1906–1992), a mathematician who became a naval officer and computer scientist during World War II, started distributing these wire "nanoseconds" in the late 1960s in order to demonstrate how designing smaller components would produce faster computers.</q></cite><span title="ctx_ver=Z39.88-2004&amp;rft_val_fmt=info%3Aofi%2Ffmt%3Akev%3Amtx%3Ajournal&amp;rft.genre=unknown&amp;rft.jtitle=National+Museum+of+American+History&amp;rft.atitle=Nanoseconds+Associated+with+Grace+Hopper&amp;rft_id=https%3A%2F%2Famericanhistory.si.edu%2Fcollections%2Fsearch%2Fobject%2Fnmah_692464&amp;rfr_id=info%3Asid%2Fen.wikipedia.org%3ASpeed+of+light" class="Z3988" about="#mwt147" id="mwCMw"></span></span></li>
